**Parallel Routes** in Next.js allow you to render multiple pages or route segments simultaneously within the same layout using named slots (`@folderName`).

### Use Cases of Parallel Routes

* Display multiple independent sections on the same page.
* Build dashboards with separate panels/widgets.
* Show side-by-side content like:

  * latest news + archived news
  * analytics + notifications
  * chat list + chat window
* Create modal routes without leaving the current page.
* Load different parts of the UI independently for better user experience.

Your folder structure should look like this:

```txt
app
 └── archive
      ├── @archive
      │     └── page.js
      ├── @latest
      │     └── page.js
      └── layout.js
```

And your `layout.js` can receive both slots as props:

```jsx
import React from 'react'

function ArchiveLayout({ archive, latest }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>News Archive</h1>

      <section id="archive-filter">
        {archive}
      </section>

      <section id="archive-latest">
        {latest}
      </section>
    </div>
  )
}

export default ArchiveLayout
```

A few important clarifications:

* `@archive` and `@latest` are **parallel route slots**, not actual URL segments.
* The URL will still be `/archive`.
* Each slot renders its own `page.js`.
* The slot name (`archive`, `latest`) becomes the prop name in `layout.js`.

Example:

```txt
@archive  → archive prop
@latest   → latest prop
```

So this part is correct:

```jsx
function ArchiveLayout({ archive, latest })
```

One thing to remember:
If you want a default UI when a slot is inactive, you should add a `default.js` inside the slot folder.

Example:

```txt
@archive
   ├── page.js
   └── default.js
```

Otherwise Next.js may show a 404 for unmatched slots during navigation.

