**Route Groups:

In Next.js, Route Groups allow you to organize your route segments and project files into logical folders without affecting the public URL path structure.
To create a route group, you wrap a folder's name in parentheses: (folderName).

Key Features of Route Groups
Omitted from the URL: The folder name with parentheses is completely ignored by Next.js when generating URLs.
Organize Code Logically: You can group related pages (e.g., marketing pages, authentication pages, dashboard pages) together.
Apply Shared Layouts selectively: You can apply a layout to only a specific subset of routes by placing a layout.js inside the route group.
Create Multiple Root Layouts: You can partition your application into completely different root layouts (e.g., one layout for the website and another for the dashboard app) by deleting the top-level app/layout.js and creating individual root layouts inside different route groups.

Concrete Examples
Example 1: Organizing folders without changing the URL
If you want to group your login and registration pages together under an auth folder, but you don't want (/auth) to appear in the URL:

text
app/
├── (auth)/
│   ├── login/
│   │   └── page.js     --> Accessible at: /login  (Not /auth/login)
│   └── register/
│       └── page.js     --> Accessible at: /register  (Not /auth/register)
Example 2: Selective Layouts (Using multiple layouts)
Suppose you want your marketing pages (/about, /contact) to share a navigation header, but your user dashboard page (/dashboard) to have a sidebar:

text
app/
├── (marketing)/
│   ├── layout.js       --> Shares a common layout (e.g., Navigation Header)
│   ├── about/
│   │   └── page.js     --> URL: /about (uses marketing layout)
│   └── contact/
│       └── page.js     --> URL: /contact (uses marketing layout)
│
├── (dashboard)/
│   ├── layout.js       --> Shares a dashboard layout (e.g., Sidebar Navigation)
│   └── page.js         --> URL: /dashboard (uses dashboard layout)
Summary of Route Group folder notation:
Folder name with parentheses (auth) $\rightarrow$ Route Group (Hidden from the URL path).
Folder name without parentheses auth $\rightarrow$ Standard Route segment (Visible in the URL path, e.g., /auth/login).

